d monthly CPI increased 0.4% in September.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"fetch_source call-0011, The Daily Consumer Price Index August 2026\",\"result\":\"The Daily metadata says CPI rose 3.0% year over year in August, matching 3.0% in July, and seasonally adjusted monthly CPI increased 0.2% in August.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"calculation\",\"call\":\"calculate calls call-0023 through call-0028 using official WDS index values\",\"result\":\"Computed same-variant annual rates from Table 18-10-0004-01: 2026-04 = 2.8, 2026-05 = 3.2, 2026-06 = 2.8, 2026-07 = 3.0, 2026-08 = 3.0, 2026-09 = 3.1.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Reference class and base rate: for this monthly all-items CPI annual-rate series, the recent official reference class is the sequence 2.3, 1.8, 2.4, 2.8, 3.2, 2.8, 3.0, 3.0, 3.1 from January through September 2026, centered just below 3 percent and rising modestly into September.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior is September's 3.1% annual rate from official WDS/The Daily history; historical sample is January-September 2026 annual rates [2.3, 1.8, 2.4, 2.8, 3.2, 2.8, 3.0, 3.0, 3.1]. Level effect: September index 170.0 is already 2.8% above November 2025's 165.4 denominator. Momentum effect: two more monthly prints around a combined +0.5 index point imply a November 2026 index near 170.8. One-off/policy-mechanism effects: food and shelter persistence offset possible energy/goods disinflation. Interval method: call-0017 gives sigma = 0.389 from successive changes in the historical annual-rate series; call-0020 gives 1.28*sigma = 0.498, so around a 3.3 point estimate the 80% bounds round to 2.8 and 3.8.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Point calculation: using a November 2026 index judgment of 170.8 and the official November 2025 base index of 165.4, call-0019 computes ((170.8 / 165.4) - 1) * 100 = 3.3 after one-decimal rounding.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: upside risk is a stronger October-November rebound in gasoline, rent, mortgage interest cost, or food that would land above the interval. Downside risk is flat or falling energy plus faster goods disinflation that would land below the interval. A large tax, administered-price, or energy shock would be outside the interval rather than normal monthly noise.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":3.3,\"ciLow\":2.8,\"ciHigh\":3.8}]}"}}
